WEBVTT


00:00:01.153 --> 00:00:06.153
Hello, welcome back to the course, today we are going to continue talking about text formatting.

00:00:05.854 --> 00:00:13.854
Let us start from this general rules of the syntax. So, for example, to type

00:00:19.687 --> 00:00:22.687
text, we just type plain text. If we'd like to have our text in italics, then we use this single asterisk and single underscores.

00:00:22.515 --> 00:00:32.515
If you would like to have our text in bold, then we use double asterisk or double underscores, and then we have also other options for

00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:40.123
the syntax of our text, but I think what's most important here is how to

00:00:42.391 --> 00:00:45.391
create a link with the text

00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:54.106
associated with that link. So basically I put the text in the bracket, and then I put the link into parentheses, and you can see that now I have a text

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:03.993
with the link associated with it. When it comes to creating headers, we basically use hashtags, and the more hashtags we use,

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:10.607
the smaller the header becomes. And, for example, for creating this particular header

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.650
in this slide, I used two hashtags. When it comes to the creation of an unordered list,

00:01:19.297 --> 00:01:25.297
we could first select a symbol we would like to use. For example, I would like to start from an asterisk,

00:01:24.374 --> 00:01:34.374
or I would like to start from a plus, we could basically swap them. And then what's important when it comes to an unordered list is the spacing from

00:01:34.486 --> 00:01:41.486
the beginning of the line. So the spacing for elements of the same list should be the same. So you can see that

00:01:42.362 --> 00:01:51.362
the symbols here are basically on the same level, and then the symbols, like pluses here, are also, like, spaced equally from the beginning of the line.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:01.067
When it comes to ordered lists or definition lists, it's very intuitive, I just start typing the numbers or the letters.

00:02:01.013 --> 00:02:06.013
And once again, the spacing is important, the spacing from the beginning of the line.

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:11.829
And then for the definition lists, first I defined

00:02:12.112 --> 00:02:19.112
that definition, what that is, so remark, term, etc. And then I write the text of the definition.

00:02:23.851 --> 00:02:26.851
Alright, when it comes to the creation of tables, we have these vertical bars and dashes to create them.

00:02:28.789 --> 00:02:33.789
So the number of dashes defines the width of a particular column.

00:02:33.969 --> 00:02:45.969
And then, alignment is defined through placing colons. So basically, if the colon is placed on the right, the text is aligned to the right.

00:02:45.337 --> 00:02:51.337
Similarly, if the colon is placed to the left, the text is aligned to the left.

00:02:54.151 --> 00:02:57.151
By default, we have left alignment.

00:02:57.179 --> 00:03:03.179
When it comes to embedding mathematical formulas, this can be done with LaTeX.

00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:15.567
So, for example, we could put our formula in that two dollar signs, and then our formula will appear in the next line.

00:03:16.046 --> 00:03:21.046
If we would like to have an inline formula, then we could use just single dollars,

00:03:21.252 --> 00:03:25.252
and then inside the formula between them. You can see now, that

00:03:29.148 --> 00:03:32.148
that formula is inserted inline to our text. And here, you have a link, with a math

00:03:40.448 --> 00:03:43.448
cheat sheet, there's one hint I would like to give you. So, if you know LaTeX, then you can embed LaTeX commands in R Markdown.

00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:49.393
But this works properly when generating PDFs, but not for HTMLs.

00:03:50.507 --> 00:03:56.507
We could also insert slide breaks and footnotes using these three asterisks.

00:03:56.635 --> 00:04:02.635
And we could also create footnotes and remarks. So to create a footnote or remark,

00:04:02.479 --> 00:04:08.479
we used this bracket, and then caret, and then the number of the

00:04:13.846 --> 00:04:16.846
footnote. When it comes to the

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:27.250
text input and output for embedding R: Let's just have a look. So basically here, this is how I would

00:04:24.393 --> 00:04:30.393
write an R chunk, so you can see, if I write it in this way,

00:04:39.199 --> 00:04:44.199
then I can see this line of text, and I can also see it in the console. So you can see that this is also written as a character in the console.

00:04:43.593 --> 00:04:49.593
If I would like to avoid having text from the script

00:04:51.835 --> 00:04:55.835
written, then I need to select echo parameter to be false.

00:05:04.534 --> 00:05:06.534
Now, how can we embed a plot into our R chunk? So basically, here you can see that I

00:05:04.875 --> 00:05:08.875
started my R chunk, then I selected the height of the plot,

00:05:08.974 --> 00:05:13.974
and basically, I selected the background to be light gray.

00:05:14.215 --> 00:05:20.215
And then here you can see that I just used our plot function to create that plot, and you can see that

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.957
this is something I can see now in my R Markdown document embedded.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:32.670
When it comes to creation of the tables from R,

00:05:40.575 --> 00:05:43.575
it's possible, so basically here we have this tab, which is basically a table for the species from IRIS data, and then I could create a table using this

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:56.151
kable function from knitr package. And here we can also customize, which particular element we would like to display in our table.

00:05:56.714 --> 00:06:01.714
And here is a list of some chunk options.

00:06:01.841 --> 00:06:10.841
Instead of me going through all these options, I definitely advise you to try them yourself.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:17.790
Because some of them will be more useful for you, some of them will be less useful for you, but definitely,

00:06:17.886 --> 00:06:22.886
you know, try it out, have a look, and have fun while programming.

